  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Monday)\nIssued at 1152 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nLate morning GOES water vapor imagery showed a weak southern\nbranch upper level trough over the Red River Valley. Up north,\nthe main northern branch of westerlies extended from the Pacific\nNorthwest to the Mid-Atlantic coast.\n\nFor the remainder of today, mid and high clouds will stream into\nthe region, ahead of the southern branch trough. This trough will\nshear eastward tonight, in advance of a northern branch trough\napproaching from the north. A few high-based showers will be\npossible southwest of Memphis toward morning. Otherwise, this\nsouthern branch trough will have little impact on the Midsouth.\n\nThe main feature of concern remains cold frontal passage late\nWednesday afternoon and evening. This front will pass through in\ntandem with a northern branch shortwave. While surface-based CAPE\nwill be more than adequate (800 J/kg) for thunder over West\nTennessee, the most favorable frontal convergence will set up\nnorth east of the forecast area, nearer to the Ohio River. Rain\namounts over the Midsouth will prevail below 0.10 inch, and\nlimited to areas east of the Mississippi River late Wednesday\nafternoon and evening.\n\nA second northern branch trough will dive south from the Upper\nMidwest on Thursday, reinforcing a longwave trough over the\neastern third of the CONUS. Under strong northwest flow aloft,\nsteady surface pressure rises and steep low level lapse rates\nwill keep wind speeds elevated over the Midsouth through Friday\nafternoon. Surface winds will finally diminish Friday night, as\nthe upper flow deamplifies. A combination of light winds, clear\nskies and dry air will result in our first widespread hard freeze\n(low temps in the mid 20s), particularly east of the Mississippi\nRiver.\n\nDespite shortwave ridging aloft, Saturday's high temperatures\nwill average 10 degrees below normal, under increasing high\nclouds. These high clouds suggest Sunday morning won't be quite\nas cold as Saturday along and west of the Mississippi River.\n\nNext week will bring a gradual warming trend. An upper ridge will\ntransit the lower and middle Mississippi River Valley early in\nthe week, followed by southwest flow aloft and returning rain\nchances late next week. By late next week, low temperatures may\nbe as warm as this weekend's high temperatures.",
